<%# locals: (f:, article:) %>
<%#
  Mounts the documentation editor over the `content` field.

  It uses the Super Admin Vue harness that is already loaded on every Administrate page -- `render_vue_component`
  (app/controllers/super_admin/application_controller.rb:33-42) emits the `#app` element that
  app/javascript/entrypoints/superadmin_pages.js mounts -- so there is no new entrypoint and no new asset
  pipeline. Orchestrator ruling R3 point 1 named this harness for exactly this.

  The component name below is one half of a contract: the other half is the `ComponentMapping` key in that
  entrypoint, and a divergence between them renders nothing at all rather than failing. Both halves are pinned --
  this string in spec/controllers/super_admin/documentation_editor_spec.rb, the registry lookup in
  app/javascript/entrypoints/superadmin_pages.spec.js.

  Every string is passed in from en.yml; the component renders no copy of its own. The keys are camelised here so
  the locale file stays snake_case Rails and the component stays camelCase JavaScript, with one name per string
  and no mapping table.
%>
<%= render_vue_component(
      'DocumentationArticleEditor',
      content: article.content.to_s,
      articleId: article.id,
      locale: article.locale.presence || Documentation::Library::DEFAULT_LOCALE,
      fieldName: "#{f.object_name}[content]",
      fieldId: "#{f.object_name}_content",
      portalFieldName: "#{f.object_name}[portal_id]",
      previewEndpoint: preview_markdown_super_admin_articles_path,
      mediaEndpoint: super_admin_documentation_media_path,
      labels: t('documentation.super_admin.editor').transform_keys { |key| key.to_s.camelize(:lower) }
    ) %>
